# Cabane — environment. Optional: `docker compose up` works with no
# `.env` at all. Copy this to `.env` only to change a default.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# The only outbound traffic on a running instance is your agent's LLM calls,
# plus telemetry if you turn it on below.

# --- Secrets: made for you ------------------------------------------------------

# On its first start the app makes a signing secret (for signed file and image
# URLs) and a credential key (which seals the keys people paste into
# Connections), and keeps both on the `secrets` volume. Leave these blank.
#
# Set one only to use a value you already have: a value here wins over the
# kept one. Once set, keep it — a new signing secret breaks links already
# issued, and a new credential key makes every stored connection key unreadable.
VISION_URL_SIGNING_SECRET=
CABANE_CREDENTIAL_KEY=

# Rotating the signing secret without dropping live URLs is a two-step change.
# (1) move the old value here, put a new one in VISION_URL_SIGNING_SECRET,
# restart — tokens signed either way verify, new ones use the new secret.
# (2) once in-flight tokens have aged out (vision URLs are the long pole: one
# turn per live conversation), clear this and restart again. Leave empty otherwise.
CABANE_SIGNING_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# --- Telemetry: off unless you set it ------------------------------------------

# CT1714: send this install's own logs and measurements to an OpenTelemetry
# collector — OTLP over HTTP, to the collector's base URL. Unset (the default),
# nothing is sent and no connection is made. TRUST.md lists every field sent.
# CABANE_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318
# Sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, if your collector wants one.
# CABANE_TELEMETRY_TOKEN=
# `safe` (default) sends only fields fit to share with someone else; `full`
# adds client IP and credential id, for your own backend.
# CABANE_TELEMETRY_LEVEL=safe
# Names this install on every record. Unset → one is made once and kept.
# CABANE_TELEMETRY_INSTALL_ID=

# --- Sign-up: by invitation unless you open it --------------------------------

# Unset (the default): after the first account, people join by an invite link
# made in Settings → Invites. `open`: anyone who reaches the app can create an
# account. Restart to apply (README: Opening sign-up).
# SIGNUP_MODE=open

# The privacy notice the account-creation screens link to. Unset: no link.
# PRIVACY_NOTICE_URL=https://example.com/privacy

# --- Email: off unless you set it ------------------------------------------------

# Unset (the default): nothing is emailed, and a password-reset or email-change
# link is printed to the app's log. Set a Resend API key and the address mail
# comes from, on a domain verified in Resend, and those links are emailed
# instead (README: Adding email). EMAIL_FROM is required once the key is set.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM="Cabane <cabane@example.com>"
# Where replies go, if not to EMAIL_FROM.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=

# --- On a domain: off unless you set both -------------------------------------

# Set both to put this instance on https at two hostnames you own: the app on
# one, rendered pages on the other. Point each at this machine with a DNS A
# (and AAAA) record, with ports 80 and 443 open to the internet; the `edge`
# service then gets both certificates itself. Hostnames only, no https://.
# One without the other is refused. With these set, PUBLIC_BASE_URL and PORT
# below are not used for the address; the app is reachable only through the edge.
# CABANE_DOMAIN=cabane.example.com
# CABANE_PAGES_DOMAIN=pages.cabane.example.com

# Optional: where the edge listens on this machine, if 80/443 are taken here by
# a proxy you already run. That proxy then forwards port 80's ACME challenges
# and re-encrypts 443 to these, for both hostnames (README: On a domain).
# EDGE_HTTP_PORT=80
# EDGE_HTTPS_PORT=443

# Optional memory limits per container (e.g. 2g), for a machine shared with
# other work. Unset: no limit.
# APP_MEMORY_LIMIT=
# DB_MEMORY_LIMIT=
# EDGE_MEMORY_LIMIT=
# The built-in runner's, shared by every turn on it (README: What the built-in
# runner doesn't promise).
# COMPANION_MEMORY_LIMIT=

# --- The built-in runner -------------------------------------------------------

# 1 (default): a companion container runs your agents (README: Give your agents
# somewhere to run). 0: leave it out, and run agents on companions paired from
# other machines. The instance works either way.
# BUILT_IN_RUNNER=1

# --- Each turn in a container of its own: off unless you add the file --------

# Lay compose.turns.yaml over compose.yaml on every command, without typing it
# (README: Each turn in a container of its own). Compose reads this itself.
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.turns.yaml
# The group of Docker's socket, so the runner can start turns' containers:
# `stat -c %g /var/run/docker.sock`. Required with compose.turns.yaml.
# DOCKER_GID=
# Each turn's memory in MB, processors and processes.
# TURN_MEMORY_MB=4096
# TURN_CPUS=2
# TURN_PIDS=512
# Turns running at the same time; the rest wait.
# MAX_CONCURRENT_TURNS=4
# A control group (or systemd slice) of yours every turn's container starts
# under, so your own limit on it caps them all. Unset: none.
# TURN_CGROUP_PARENT=
# `closed` (default): a turn's only route out is the egress proxy. `open` adds
# a direct route out.
# TURN_NETWORK=closed
# The egress proxy's memory limit (e.g. 256m). Unset: no limit.
# EGRESS_MEMORY_LIMIT=

# A companion on another machine, with compose.companion.yaml: the instance's
# https address, and the device's name in the app (default cabane-companion).
# The turn settings above apply to it too.
# CABANE_BASE_URL=https://cabane.example.com
# COMPANION_NAME=cabane-companion
# Plain http to this one host on a private network, instead of https.
# CABANE_ALLOW_PLAIN_HTTP_HOST=

# --- Usually leave as-is -------------------------------------------------------

# The origin you open in the browser. Must match the address bar exactly, scheme
# included. Over plain http-localhost the session cookie is (correctly) not
# Secure, so login works (CT449). If you publish on a different host/port, change
# this AND the PORT below to match.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8788

# Host port the app is published on (the in-container port is fixed at 8788).
PORT=8788

# Postgres credentials for the bundled db container. Local-only — the db isn't
# published outside the compose network — so the defaults are fine.
POSTGRES_USER=cabane
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=cabane
POSTGRES_DB=cabane
